\begin{abstract}
We develop a tropical (min-plus) model of the accuracy cost of ablating layers in
a trained deep network, and use it to explain a striking empirical phenomenon: in
a $24$-layer transformer subjected to a per-layer sparsification budget, layers
$22$ and $23$ each cost $0.03$ accuracy points when pruned alone, yet cost $0.42$
points when pruned together --- a seven-fold super-additive blow-up --- while other
pairs in the same network are exactly additive or strictly sub-additive.

The model represents a network as a finite family of \emph{computation paths},
each depending on a set of layers (its \emph{support}) and carrying a loss.
Pruning a layer set $S$ destroys every path whose support meets $S$; the
network's post-ablation loss is the minimum loss among survivors, and the
\emph{pruning cost} of $S$ is the increase of that minimum. This is literally a
sum in the tropical semiring $(\mathbb{Q}\cup\{\infty\},\min,+)$ over the
surviving paths.

We prove: (i) a \emph{representation theorem} --- a cost profile is realizable
if and only if it is monotone and vanishes at the empty set, so monotonicity is
the only universal law, no additivity law holds, and super-additivity is
unbounded; (ii) an \emph{interaction calculus} --- M\"obius inversion on the
subset lattice decomposes every joint cost into pure interactions, whose
second-order term \emph{is} pairwise epistasis and whose third order yields an
exact compounding law for triples; (iii) a \emph{combinatorial characterization}
--- an ablation costs more than $\varepsilon$ exactly when it is a transversal of
the $\varepsilon$-near-optimal path family, so the epistasis order is a hypergraph
transversal number, equal to $2$ exactly when all singletons are cheap and some
pair is not, and realizable at any prescribed value; and (iv) a \emph{structural
additivity criterion} --- a two-path merge axiom forces
$\cost(S\cup T)\le\max(\cost(S),\cost(T))$ and makes per-layer budgeting provably
safe, while a single super-additive pair yields an explicit merge obstruction.
Finally we exhibit a $20$-path system on $24$ layers reproducing the measured
ablation table exactly, turning the experiment's verdicts into theorems,
including the exact decomposition $0.76 = 0.19 + 0.94 - 0.37$ of the tail-triple
cost into solo, pairwise and third-order interactions.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} tropical semiring, min-plus algebra, layer ablation,
epistasis, M\"obius inversion, hypergraph transversal, network pruning,
co-adaptation.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measurement}

Consider a trained transformer of depth $L=24$. Impose a sparsification budget of
$k=16$ retained components per layer, apply it to one layer at a time, and measure
held-out accuracy loss with no retraining. The resulting \emph{solo cost profile}
assigns to each layer $i$ a number $c(\{i\})\ge 0$. Applying the same budget to
several layers simultaneously and comparing against the sum of solo costs yields
the following six arms. Costs are in accuracy points; internally we work in
hundredths of a point so that all quantities are integers.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{llrrrl}
\toprule
arm & layers & joint cost & $\sum$ solo & ratio & class \\
\midrule
tail        & $\{22,23\}$    & $0.42$ & $0.06$ & $7.00$ & \textbf{super-additive} \\
bulk        & $\{12,15\}$    & $0.60$ & $0.79$ & $0.76$ & sub-additive \\
front       & $\{0,1\}$      & $0.25$ & $0.25$ & $1.00$ & additive \\
mid         & $\{10,11\}$    & $0.40$ & $0.28$ & $1.43$ & super-additive \\
cross       & $\{12,22\}$    & $0.59$ & $0.60$ & $0.98$ & sub-additive \\
tail triple & $\{21,22,23\}$ & $0.76$ & $0.19$ & $4.00$ & \textbf{super-additive} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The underlying solo profile is
\[
c(\{0\})=0.13,\quad c(\{1\})=0.12,\quad c(\{10\})=c(\{11\})=0.14,
\]
\[
c(\{12\})=0.57,\quad c(\{15\})=0.22,\quad c(\{21\})=0.13,\quad c(\{22\})=c(\{23\})=0.03 .
\]

Three pre-registered hypotheses were tested. \textbf{P1} --- \emph{the tail pair is
simultaneously cheapest by solo sum and disproportionately costly jointly} ---
is confirmed (smallest solo sum, largest ratio). \textbf{P2} --- \emph{joint
ablation costs are essentially additive} --- is refuted (three of six arms
super-additive, two sub-additive, one additive). \textbf{P3} --- \emph{the tail
triple compounds} --- is confirmed at ratio $4$, and the triple is the costliest
arm.

\subsection{The problem, and the answer}

Two questions demand a mathematical answer. \textbf{(Q1)} What kind of object is a
pruning-cost profile: which functions on subsets of layers can arise at all?
\textbf{(Q2)} What structural invariant distinguishes the tail pair from the front
pair, given that both are adjacent pairs with small solo costs?

Model the network as a family of computation paths. Pruning destroys paths, the
network falls back on the best survivor, so post-pruning loss is a \emph{minimum}
--- a tropical sum --- and cost is the increase of that minimum. Then (Q1) has the
sharpest possible answer: every monotone normalized profile is realizable
(Theorem~\ref{thm:rep}), so monotonicity is the only law and super-additivity is
unbounded (Theorem~\ref{thm:pure}). And (Q2) has a purely combinatorial answer: an
ablation is expensive precisely when it is a hitting set of the near-optimal path
family (Theorem~\ref{thm:hit}), so the tail pair is a \emph{minimal size-two
transversal} while the front pair is a union of two independent size-one
transversals. Additivity is recovered exactly under a two-path exchange axiom
(Theorem~\ref{thm:merge}), and the tail pair furnishes an explicit obstruction to
it (Theorem~\ref{thm:obst}).

\subsection{Why tropical}

The min-plus semiring $\mathbb{T}=(\mathbb{Q}\cup\{\infty\},\oplus,\odot)$, with
$x\oplus y=\min(x,y)$ and $x\odot y=x+y$, is the natural arithmetic of
best-route problems. Post-ablation loss is
$\bigoplus_{i\ \mathrm{survives}}\mathrm{loss}(i)$, an honest tropical sum
(Proposition~\ref{prop:tropsum}). The persistent intuition that ``damage adds
up'' is the intuition of the wrong semiring: deleting two terms from a $+$-sum
removes exactly two contributions, whereas deleting two terms from a $\min$ can
remove nothing or everything, depending entirely on what lies beneath.

\section{The tropical model}

\begin{definition}[Prunable net]
Fix $n\in\mathbb{N}$, the number of layers, indexed by $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$. A
\emph{prunable net} consists of a finite index set $\iota$ of \emph{paths}; a
\emph{support} map $\supp:\iota\to\mathcal{P}(\{0,\dots,n-1\})$; a \emph{loss}
map $\mathrm{loss}:\iota\to\mathbb{Q}$; and a distinguished \emph{fallback path}
$\mathrm{base}\in\iota$ with $\supp(\mathrm{base})=\emptyset$.
\end{definition}

The fallback path encodes that even a fully pruned network computes
\emph{something}; it guarantees the survivor set is never empty.

\begin{definition}[Survivors, net loss, cost, epistasis]
For a layer set $S$ put
$\Surv(S)=\{\,i\in\iota : \supp(i)\cap S=\emptyset\,\}$ and
\[
\netLoss(S)=\min_{i\in\Surv(S)}\mathrm{loss}(i),
\qquad
\cost(S)=\netLoss(S)-\netLoss(\emptyset),
\]
\[
\epi(S,T)=\cost(S\cup T)-\cost(S)-\cost(T).
\]
The pair $(S,T)$ is \emph{super-additive} if $\epi(S,T)>0$ and
\emph{sub-additive} if $\epi(S,T)<0$.
\end{definition}

Note $\mathrm{base}\in\Surv(S)$ always, so $\Surv(S)\neq\emptyset$; and
$S\subseteq T$ implies $\Surv(T)\subseteq\Surv(S)$. Also $\epi$ is symmetric and
$\epi(S,\emptyset)=0$.

\begin{proposition}[Basic calculus]\label{prop:basic}
Let $S\subseteq T$ be layer sets.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\supp(i)\cap S=\emptyset$ then $\netLoss(S)\le\mathrm{loss}(i)$.
\item If $q\le\mathrm{loss}(i)$ for every survivor $i$ of $S$, then
      $q\le\netLoss(S)$.
\item Some survivor $i$ of $S$ attains $\mathrm{loss}(i)=\netLoss(S)$.
\item $\netLoss(S)\le\netLoss(T)$ and $\cost(S)\le\cost(T)$.
\item $\cost(\emptyset)=0$ and $\cost(S)\ge 0$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1)--(3) are the defining properties of a minimum over a nonempty finite set. (4)
follows from antitonicity of survivors: every survivor of $T$ survives $S$, so
the minimum over the smaller family is at least as large. (5) is (4) with
$S=\emptyset$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Witness rule]\label{cor:witness}
If $i_0$ survives $S$ and $\mathrm{loss}(i_0)\le\mathrm{loss}(i)$ for every
survivor $i$ of $S$, then $\netLoss(S)=\mathrm{loss}(i_0)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Net loss is a tropical sum]\label{prop:tropsum}
For every layer set $S$,
\[
\untrop\!\left(\bigoplus_{i\in\Surv(S)}\trop\big(\mathrm{loss}(i)\big)\right)=\netLoss(S).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Tropical summation over a finite index set unfolds to the infimum of the
summands. One inequality uses the attained optimal survivor of
Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(3); the other uses
Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(1) termwise.
\end{proof}

\section{Representation: which cost profiles exist?}

\begin{definition}[Canonical net]
Given $c:\mathcal{P}(\{0,\dots,n-1\})\to\mathbb{Q}$, the \emph{canonical net}
$N_c$ has paths indexed by subsets $A$, with $\supp(A)=A$,
$\mathrm{loss}(A)=c(A^{c})$, and fallback path $A=\emptyset$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:canon}
If $c$ is monotone ($S\subseteq T\Rightarrow c(S)\le c(T)$) then
$\netLoss_{N_c}(S)=c(S)$ for every $S$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $\le$: the path $A=S^{c}$ has support disjoint from $S$ and loss $c(S)$. For
$\ge$: if $A$ survives $S$ then $A\cap S=\emptyset$, so $S\subseteq A^{c}$ and
monotonicity gives $c(S)\le c(A^{c})=\mathrm{loss}(A)$; thus $c(S)$ bounds all
survivors below.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Representation]\label{thm:rep}
A function $c$ on subsets of $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$ is the pruning-cost profile of
some prunable net if and only if $c(\emptyset)=0$ and $c$ is monotone.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Necessity is Proposition~\ref{prop:basic}(4)--(5). Sufficiency: by
Lemma~\ref{lem:canon}, $\netLoss_{N_c}(S)=c(S)$ and
$\netLoss_{N_c}(\emptyset)=0$, hence $\cost_{N_c}=c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded pure epistasis]\label{thm:pure}
For distinct layers $a\neq b$ and any $r>0$ there is a prunable net with
$\cost(\{a\})=\cost(\{b\})=0$ and $\cost(\{a,b\})=r$, hence
$\epi(\{a\},\{b\})=r>0$ with unbounded ratio.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:rep} to the monotone normalized profile $c(S)=r$ if
$a,b\in S$ and $c(S)=0$ otherwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sub-additivity is equally realizable]
The threshold profile $c(S)=1$ for $S\neq\emptyset$, $c(\emptyset)=0$, is
monotone; the resulting net has
$\cost(\{a\})=\cost(\{b\})=\cost(\{a,b\})=1$, so $\epi(\{a\},\{b\})=-1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Zero-epistasis null model]\label{thm:modular}
Let $\varphi:\{0,\dots,n-1\}\to\mathbb{Q}_{\ge 0}$ and let
$c_\varphi(S)=\sum_{i\in S}\varphi(i)$. Then for disjoint $S,T$ one has
$\epi(S,T)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
Together these show that super-, sub- and exact additivity all occur inside one
model class, and Theorem~\ref{thm:modular} identifies exactly when additivity
holds: \emph{epistasis is precisely the failure of the loss landscape to be
modular}. Hypothesis P2 is refuted at the level of the whole model class.
\end{remark}

\section{The interaction calculus}

\begin{definition}[M\"obius / pure-interaction coefficient]
For a cost profile $c$ and a layer set $A$,
\[
m_c(A)=\sum_{B\subseteq A}(-1)^{|A\setminus B|}c(B).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Shift identity]\label{lem:shift}
For $x\notin A$, writing $c^{(x)}(B):=c(B\cup\{x\})$,
\[
m_c(A\cup\{x\})=m_{c^{(x)}}(A)-m_c(A).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split $\mathcal{P}(A\cup\{x\})$ into subsets containing $x$ and subsets not
containing $x$. For $B\subseteq A$ one has $|(A\cup\{x\})\setminus B|=|A\setminus
B|+1$, producing the sign flip that yields $-m_c(A)$; while
$(A\cup\{x\})\setminus(B\cup\{x\})=A\setminus B$, producing $m_{c^{(x)}}(A)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[M\"obius inversion for pruning costs]\label{thm:mobius}
For every layer set $S$, $\;c(S)=\sum_{A\subseteq S}m_c(A)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $S$ with the profile generalized. The base case is trivial. For
$S\cup\{x\}$ with $x\notin S$, split the power set, apply Lemma~\ref{lem:shift}
termwise, and use the inductive hypothesis twice --- once for $c$ and once for
$c^{(x)}$ --- to obtain $c^{(x)}(S)=c(S\cup\{x\})$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Low orders]\label{cor:orders}
With $c(\emptyset)=0$:
$m_c(\{i\})=c(\{i\})$;
$m_c(\{a,b\})=c(\{a,b\})-c(\{a\})-c(\{b\})$; and
\[
m_c(\{a,b,d\})=c(\{a,b,d\})-c(\{a,b\})-c(\{a,d\})-c(\{b,d\})+c(\{a\})+c(\{b\})+c(\{d\}).
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Epistasis is the second-order coefficient]
For distinct $a\neq b$, $\;\epi(\{a\},\{b\})=m_{\cost}(\{a,b\})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Compounding law for triples]\label{thm:triple}
For pairwise distinct $a,b,d$,
\[
c(\{a,b,d\})-\big(c(\{a\})+c(\{b\})+c(\{d\})\big)
=\epi(\{a\},\{b\})+\epi(\{a\},\{d\})+\epi(\{b\},\{d\})+m_c(\{a,b,d\}).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand the four M\"obius coefficients via Corollary~\ref{cor:orders} and cancel;
equivalently, this is Theorem~\ref{thm:mobius} at $S=\{a,b,d\}$ regrouped by
order.
\end{proof}

The naive expectation that a triple's excess is the sum of its pairwise excesses
is exactly the statement $m_c(\{a,b,d\})=0$; Section~\ref{sec:net60} shows it
fails for the measured tail triple with a \emph{negative} third-order term.

\section{Epistasis as a hitting-set number}

Fix a prunable net and a tolerance $\varepsilon\ge 0$.

\begin{definition}[Near-optimal paths; transversal]
A path $i$ is \emph{$\varepsilon$-near-optimal} if
$\mathrm{loss}(i)\le\netLoss(\emptyset)+\varepsilon$; write $\Near(\varepsilon)$
for these paths. A layer set $S$ is a \emph{transversal} of $\Near(\varepsilon)$
if $\supp(i)\cap S\neq\emptyset$ for every $i\in\Near(\varepsilon)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Hitting-set characterization of cost]\label{thm:hit}
$\cost(S)>\varepsilon$ if and only if $S$ is a transversal of
$\Near(\varepsilon)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$, contrapositive) If some $\varepsilon$-near-optimal path $i$
survives $S$ then $\netLoss(S)\le\mathrm{loss}(i)\le\netLoss(\emptyset)+
\varepsilon$, so $\cost(S)\le\varepsilon$. ($\Leftarrow$) Take the optimal
survivor $i$ of $S$; since $S$ hits every near-optimal path and $i$ survives, $i$
is not near-optimal, so $\netLoss(S)=\mathrm{loss}(i)>\netLoss(\emptyset)+
\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cheapness certificate]
If a path $i$ with $\mathrm{loss}(i)\le\netLoss(\emptyset)+\varepsilon$ has
support disjoint from $S$, then $\cost(S)\le\varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Epistasis order]
$\epiOrder(\varepsilon)=\min\{\,|S| : \cost(S)>\varepsilon\,\}$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:hit} this is the transversal number of the hypergraph whose
edges are the supports of the $\varepsilon$-near-optimal paths.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
If $|S|<\epiOrder(\varepsilon)$ then $\cost(S)\le\varepsilon$; and if some set is
expensive, then some set of cardinality exactly $\epiOrder(\varepsilon)$ is
expensive.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Order-two criterion]\label{thm:order2}
Suppose $\varepsilon\ge 0$, $\cost(\{i\})\le\varepsilon$ for every layer $i$, and
$\cost(\{a,b\})>\varepsilon$ for some pair. Then $\epiOrder(\varepsilon)=2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The pair gives $\epiOrder\le 2$. Cardinality $0$ would give
$0=\cost(\emptyset)>\varepsilon\ge 0$, absurd; cardinality $1$ contradicts the
singleton hypothesis.
\end{proof}

This is the invariant separating the two arms: the tail pair is a \emph{minimal}
size-two transversal, whereas the front pair is a union of two independent
size-one transversals, each covering its own family of near-optimal paths, whose
costs therefore add.

\begin{theorem}[Co-adaptation]\label{thm:coad}
Suppose $\cost(\{a\})\le\varepsilon$, $\cost(\{b\})\le\varepsilon$ and
$\cost(\{a,b\})>\varepsilon$. Then there are $\varepsilon$-near-optimal paths
$p,q$ with $a\notin\supp(p)$, $b\in\supp(p)$, $b\notin\supp(q)$,
$a\in\supp(q)$. If moreover $\cost(\{a\})=\cost(\{b\})=0$, then $p$ and $q$ are
exactly optimal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $p$ realize $\netLoss(\{a\})$; it avoids $a$ and is near-optimal because the
solo cost of $a$ is at most $\varepsilon$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:hit} the pair
$\{a,b\}$ hits $p$, and since $p$ avoids $a$ it must contain $b$. Swap roles for
$q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every order is realizable]\label{thm:block}
Let $K$ be a nonempty set of $k$ layers and $r>0$. The block profile $c_K(S)=r$
if $K\subseteq S$ and $0$ otherwise is monotone and normalized; the resulting net
satisfies $\epiOrder(0)=|K|=k$.
\end{theorem}

\section{When additivity holds: the merge axiom}

\begin{definition}[Mergeability]
A prunable net is \emph{mergeable} if for all paths $p,q$ there is a path $r$ with
$\supp(r)\subseteq\supp(p)\cap\supp(q)$ and
$\mathrm{loss}(r)\le\max(\mathrm{loss}(p),\mathrm{loss}(q))$.
\end{definition}

In words: any two backup routes admit a common refinement depending only on the
layers both of them need, and no worse than the worse of the two. No capability
lives in the disagreement between two routes.

\begin{theorem}[Merge bound]\label{thm:merge}
In a mergeable net, $\cost(S\cup T)\le\max(\cost(S),\cost(T))$ for all $S,T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $p$ realize $\netLoss(S)$, $q$ realize $\netLoss(T)$, and let $r$ be their
merge. Since $\supp(r)$ lies inside both supports, $r$ survives $S\cup T$, so
$\netLoss(S\cup T)\le\mathrm{loss}(r)\le\max(\mathrm{loss}(p),\mathrm{loss}(q))
=\max(\netLoss(S),\netLoss(T))$; subtract $\netLoss(\emptyset)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
In a mergeable net $\cost(S\cup T)\le\cost(S)+\cost(T)$ and $\epi(S,T)\le 0$ for
all $S,T$: no super-additive pair exists anywhere.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Per-layer budgeting is safe]\label{cor:budget}
In a mergeable net, $\cost(S)\le\max_{i\in S}\cost(\{i\})$ for every nonempty $S$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction on $|S|$ using Theorem~\ref{thm:merge}: split off one layer and bound
the remainder by the inductive hypothesis.
\end{proof}

A \emph{local} two-path exchange property thus upgrades to a bound over the entire
Boolean lattice of $2^{n}$ subsets simultaneously. Mergeability is exactly the
licence to do per-layer accounting.

\begin{theorem}[Explicit merge obstruction]\label{thm:obst}
Suppose $\epi(S,T)>0$. Then the net is not mergeable, and there exist paths $p,q$
with $p$ surviving $S$, $q$ surviving $T$, $\mathrm{loss}(p)=\netLoss(S)$,
$\mathrm{loss}(q)=\netLoss(T)$, such that \emph{every} path $r$ with
$\supp(r)\subseteq\supp(p)\cap\supp(q)$ satisfies
$\mathrm{loss}(r)>\max(\mathrm{loss}(p),\mathrm{loss}(q))$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Take $p,q$ optimal for $S$ and $T$. If some $r$ inside the intersection of
supports were no worse than both, it would survive $S\cup T$ and the computation
of Theorem~\ref{thm:merge} would force $\epi(S,T)\le 0$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:obst} converts the numerical statement ``$0.42$ against $0.06$''
into a structural one: the network holds a capability located precisely in the
disagreement between two backup routes.

