\subsection{Verdicts as theorems}

\begin{theorem}[Solo and arm profiles]
$\cost(\{0\})=13$, $\cost(\{1\})=12$, $\cost(\{10\})=\cost(\{11\})=14$,
$\cost(\{12\})=57$, $\cost(\{15\})=22$, $\cost(\{21\})=13$,
$\cost(\{22\})=\cost(\{23\})=3$; and
$\cost(\{0,1\})=25$, $\cost(\{10,11\})=40$, $\cost(\{12,15\})=60$,
$\cost(\{12,22\})=59$, $\cost(\{22,23\})=42$, $\cost(\{21,22,23\})=76$,
$\cost(\{21,22\})=\cost(\{21,23\})=45$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[P1: the tail ratio is exactly seven]
$\cost(\{22,23\})=7\big(\cost(\{22\})+\cost(\{23\})\big)$, and
$\epi(\{22\},\{23\})=36$, i.e.\ $0.36$ points of pure second-order interaction.
Moreover, among the five pair arms the tail pair has the strictly smallest solo
sum, $3+3<13+12,\;14+14,\;57+22,\;57+3$, and the strictly largest
joint-to-solo ratio (compared by cross-multiplication, so that no division is
needed).
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[P2 refuted: all three regimes in one net]
\[
\epi(\{22\},\{23\})=36>0,\quad \epi(\{10\},\{11\})=12>0,\quad \epi(\{21\},\{22,23\})=21>0,
\]
\[
\epi(\{12\},\{15\})=-19<0,\quad \epi(\{12\},\{22\})=-1<0,\quad \epi(\{0\},\{1\})=0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[P3: the triple compounds and is costliest]
$\cost(\{21,22,23\})=4\big(\cost(\{21\})+\cost(\{22\})+\cost(\{23\})\big)=76$, and
$76$ strictly exceeds each of $25,40,60,59,42$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Co-adaptation of the tail pair]
At tolerance $\varepsilon=3$ the tail pair is a transversal of the near-optimal
path family: there is a near-optimal path avoiding layer $22$ but routing through
layer $23$, and one avoiding $23$ but routing through $22$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:coad} with $\cost(\{22\})=\cost(\{23\})=3\le 3<42$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Epistasis order]
Every single layer satisfies $\cost(\{i\})\le 57$ while $\cost(\{12,15\})=60>57$,
so $\epiOrder(57)=2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:order2}. In the tail subsystem --- the
$5$-path net retaining $\emptyset,\{22\},\{23\}$, all layers outside the tail, and
everything, with losses $0,3,3,3,42$ --- every single layer costs at most $3$ while
$\cost(\{22,23\})=42$, so $\epiOrder(3)=2$: budgets must be assigned to the pair,
never to its members.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Not mergeable]
Since $\epi(\{22\},\{23\})=36>0$, the measured net is not mergeable, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:obst} produces the explicit obstruction: the optimal backup
avoiding layer $22$ (loss $3$) and the optimal backup avoiding layer $23$ (loss
$3$) admit no common refinement --- every route depending only on the layers both
of them require costs strictly more than $3$.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Third-order structure: the unit saturates at width two}

\begin{theorem}[Third-order interaction and exact decomposition]
\[
m_{\cost}(\{21,22,23\})=76-45-45-42+13+3+3=-37,
\]
and consequently
\[
\underbrace{76}_{\text{measured}}
=\underbrace{(13+3+3)}_{\text{solo}=19}
+\underbrace{(29+29+36)}_{\text{pairwise}=94}
+\underbrace{(-37)}_{\text{third order}} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:triple} applied to $\{21,22,23\}$, using
$\epi(\{21\},\{22\})=45-13-3=29$, $\epi(\{21\},\{23\})=29$ and
$\epi(\{22\},\{23\})=36$.
\end{proof}

The third-order term is negative and large relative to the solo sum: the three
pairwise interactions over-count what a triple ablation actually costs, and the
genuine order-3 term corrects them downwards. The tail's co-adaptation therefore
does not compound indefinitely; it \emph{saturates}. The observed fourfold
compounding of the triple is compatible with a width-two co-adapted core (layers
$22,23$) into which layer $21$ is only partially recruited --- a falsifiable
prediction: if in a deeper model the core widens to three, the corresponding
third-order coefficient should turn positive.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Tropical evaluation of a cost profile.}
To evaluate $\cost(S)$, scan the paths, keep those whose support misses $S$, and
return the minimum loss less the baseline. With bitmask supports this is $O(P)$
machine-word operations for $P$ paths; verifying an ablation table of $A$ arms
costs $O(AP)$.

\paragraph{B. M\"obius transform and interaction spectrum.}
Given a cost profile on a block $K$ of $k$ layers ($2^{k}$ values), all pure
interactions $m(A)$, $A\subseteq K$, are computed by the fast zeta/M\"obius
transform in $O(k2^{k})$ time and $O(2^{k})$ space: sweep one coordinate at a
time, replacing the value at each set containing $x$ by its difference with the
value at the set without $x$. This is the butterfly of the fast
Walsh--Hadamard transform with subtraction in place of $\pm$ combination; for a
tail triple it is seven subtractions.

\paragraph{C. Epistasis order by transversal search.}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:hit}, computing $\epiOrder(\varepsilon)$ is computing the
transversal number of the hypergraph of near-optimal supports. Exhaustive search
over subsets of size $1,2,\dots$ costs $O(\binom{L}{k}P)$ to certify order $k$;
for $k\le 2$ and $L=24$ this is a few hundred evaluations. The general
transversal-number problem is NP-hard, which is exactly why the order-two
criterion (Theorem~\ref{thm:order2}) is valuable: it certifies the answer from
$O(L^{2})$ measurements without any search, replacing a hopeless $2^{L}$ subset
sweep.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Scope.}
The model is a representation result, not a mechanistic account of transformers.
It says: whatever the internal mechanism, if post-ablation performance is the
best achievable over surviving computational routes, then the cost profile is an
arbitrary monotone function, super-additivity is a hitting-set phenomenon, and
additivity is equivalent to a merge property of the route family. The twenty-path
system of Section~\ref{sec:net60} is an exact realization of the measured
profile, guaranteed to exist by Theorem~\ref{thm:rep}; its role is to make the
verdicts checkable statements about a finite transparent object.

\paragraph{Consistency with correlational markers.}
The causal signature identified here --- the tail as a single non-mergeable unit
--- matches four independently observed correlational markers of the same layers:
that the tail's structure is far from the piecewise-linear extreme regime
characteristic of the bulk; that a crystallization-style loss is concentrated
there; that decision divergence under perturbation is largest there; and that
tail structure is least portable across models. Under the present model these are
not four facts but one: a co-adapted unit is a minimal transversal of the
near-optimal route family, and each marker is a symptom of having no single-layer
backup.

\paragraph{Prescription.}
Corollary~\ref{cor:budget} read contrapositively is exact: per-layer budget
accounting is valid \emph{precisely} under mergeability; the tail pair certifies
that mergeability fails; therefore per-layer accounting is invalid for the tail.
Treat the last two layers as one unit for bits and budgets and never differentiate
between its members. For the sub-additive bulk and cross arms, per-layer
accounting is conservative and safe.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
One model, one depth, one context length, one granularity $k=16$, and five chosen
pairs out of $\binom{24}{2}=276$; the arms were pre-selected, not swept. The solo
baseline is inherited from a prior profile of the same model under the same
budget. The theory is exact but the empirical scope is narrow, and the central
conjecture --- that co-adapted units are contiguous blocks whose width grows with
depth --- is untested.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Transversal-number depth law]
For a depth-$L$ transformer the epistasis order at a fixed tolerance grows like
the width of the \emph{deepest} co-adapted block, and this block is an interval of
layers: there is a partition of $\{0,\dots,L-1\}$ into intervals such that the
near-optimal path hypergraph is the union of the ``all-of-a-block'' hyperedges of
the partition.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that a co-adapted unit is not a statistical cluster but a
minimal transversal of the near-optimal path family, and Theorem~\ref{thm:block}
shows minimal transversals of any size are tropically realizable --- the empirical
question is only whether the realized ones are contiguous. The hitting-set
characterization turns a costly ablation sweep into a hypergraph-covering
question testable with $O(L^{2})$ pair ablations plus a single fit, instead of
$2^{L}$ subsets.

\begin{conjecture}[Merge-axiom certification of prunability]
A layer-wise pruning budget is safe (per-layer accounting is valid) if and only if
the induced path system satisfies the merge axiom up to an additive slack
$\delta$, and the total super-additivity of a network is bounded by
$(\text{number of layers})\cdot\delta$.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that Corollary~\ref{cor:budget} upgrades a local two-path
exchange property into a global bound on every subset at once, so a finite
certificate implies a statement over the whole Boolean lattice. The
$\delta$-relaxed merge axiom is checkable from pairwise data already collected,
and would give the first certificate of safe per-layer budgeting rather than a
heuristic.

Beyond these: replication at larger scale to test whether tail units widen with
depth; a search for deeper-tail units guided by the transversal criterion; a
hybrid criterion combining structural probes with recency-based importance; and
evaluation on domain-jump corpora, where the near-optimal path family --- and
hence the transversal structure --- may reorganize.

\section{Conclusion}

Ablation cost is a tropical quantity: the increase of a minimum over surviving
computation routes. Once that is recognized, the empirical surprise dissolves into
three theorems. Monotonicity is the only universal constraint on cost profiles, so
additivity was never guaranteed. An ablation is expensive exactly when it hits
every near-optimal route, so the order at which epistasis lives is a hypergraph
transversal number. And additivity holds exactly when backup routes can be merged,
so a super-additive pair certifies that the network stores a capability in the
disagreement between two routes. The last two layers of the measured network are a
minimal size-two transversal of their own near-optimal route family: each
individually free because the other covers, and jointly worth seven times their
combined solo price. They are not two components; they are one.

